<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song Dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Northern Song, Southern Song, Song</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Song Dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 960 to 1279, first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in the north and then, after the Jin conquest of the north, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> in the south. It saw economic growth, urbanisation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons, along with a civil service recruited by examination. It was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-dynasty.jpg" alt="Song Dynasty" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mozzan; derivative work by Kanguole · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_-_Song_Dynasty-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The dynasty was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Emperor Taizu of Song</a>, who reigned from 960 to 976. He usurped the throne of the Later Zhou dynasty and spent sixteen years conquering the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> proper, ending the period of the Five Dynasties and Ten Kingdoms. The Song often came into conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>, Western Xia and Jin dynasties in the north. In 1127, after the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty took the northern half of Song territory, the court retreated south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and set up its capital at Lin'an, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>. The two periods are called the Northern Song, 960 to 1127, and the Southern Song, 1127 to 1279.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered the Jin in 1234 and kept uneasy relations with the Southern Song. Möngke Khan died in 1259 while besieging Diaoyucheng in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in 1271. After two decades of sporadic warfare, his armies defeated the Southern Song at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a> in 1279 and reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Yuan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Civil service examinations had existed since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>, but under the Song they became virtually the only way into government. Candidates for the lower prefectural examinations rose from about 30,000 a year in the early 11th century to 400,000 by the late 13th century. The change favoured a scholar-bureaucratic elite over a military-aristocratic one. Historians disagree about how open the system was: some, using Song statistics, argue that an official ancestor did not guarantee office, while others criticise that model for ignoring the extended family. The army was organised so that it could not threaten the throne, and the court sometimes removed or executed successful generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a>.</p><p>The Northern Song census recorded 20 million households, and its population is estimated at 90 million. Expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation, including early-ripening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa-rice">Champa rice</a> introduced in 1011, supported the growth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> each had over a million people. The state supported retirement homes, public clinics and paupers' graveyards, and its overseas trade passed through ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wujing-zongyao">Wujing Zongyao</a> of 1044 was the first book to give formulas for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> and its uses in bombs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a> was the first to discern magnetic declination while experimenting with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bi-sheng">Bi Sheng</a>'s movable type was first described in Shen's Dream Pool Essays of 1088. The Song also issued the first true <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> and set up the first permanent standing navy of a Chinese government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheng-yi-philosopher">Cheng Yi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> renewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> with new commentary, and their work established the doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Song dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A trading state on the northwest coast of Borneo appears in Chinese records from the tenth century under the name Po-ni, which sent missions to the <b>Song</b> court, and in 1365 the Javanese poem Nagarakretagama listed Barune among the dependencies of Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The most important was eaglewood, the one product Marco Polo mentioned; in 1155 a Cham envoy shipped 55,020 catties, about 33 tons, of incense to the <b>Song</b> court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign trade passed through the port of Byeongnando near Kaegyong, visited by <b>Song</b> Chinese, Japanese, Khitan and Jurchen traders and, in the eleventh century, by Arab merchants, through whom the name Goryeo probably travelled west to become Korea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1127 Jin armies carried off two <b>Song</b> emperors, Huizong and his son Qinzong, to Manchuria, where both died in captivity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1281 some 50,000 Mongol, Korean and Chinese troops and about 100,000 conscripts from the defeated <b>Song</b> fought for some seven weeks in Kyushu without strategic gain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The annual payments of silver and silk from the <b>Song dynasty</b> after 1005, together with frontier markets where horses, sheep and furs were exchanged for tea, textiles and grain, gave the court great wealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
